/** \page "Platinum Reference Documentation"
<b>Discover the Platinum C++ Framework</b>
\n
\n
Boost your daily performance with the Platinum C++ framework! With %Pt your
applications are portable to many platforms without change. Its clean and
concise design assists you in writing robust and highly reusable code.

<b>Unleash the power of %Pt and C++ onto your programs today!</b>

- \b Portablility \n
Platinum is extremely portable and runs on everything from embedded devices up
to large servers. The supported platforms include Windows, Windows CE,
Linux, QNX, Unix with many more to come. %Pt lets you spend your time on
your application taking away the pain of low-level programming.

- \b Performance \n
A high degree of performance is achieved through the use of modern C++
features. Your program will benefit from the many optimizations
included in the framework.

- <b>Easy to use</b> \n
Platinum is designed from the ground up to be flexible and extensible.
It provide simple solutions to your problems. Programs written with
Platinum tend to be significantly shorter and much easier to understand.

- <b>Open Source</b> \n
Its not just open, its free! Published under the LGPL it allows you
to write commercial application and be part of the great community that
develops PPR and determines its future.

<b>News<\b>
\n
| #05-02-2007, }Sprint Results}#\n
This slightly longer development sprint has lead to the TR-1 release. Many improvements have been made to the core module and the %Gfx module. A new module for network programming was added (%Pt::Net). %Pt can now be built wit the intel compiler on win32. The code is available as a tarball in the download section or as a tag in the subversion repository (tags/tr1).

| #01-12-2006, }Sprint Results}#\n
During the last sprint, enhancements have been made to the %Gfx module and the core module, Most notably to the image classes and the reflection API. A new module for unit testing was added. %Pt::Unit allows protocol- and data-driven testing.

| #01-11-2006, }Scrum project management introduced}#\n
The scrum system can now be used by developers to coordinate their efforts to
improve %Pt. It is not mandatory to take part in the scrum process, but highly
encouraged. Progress can be monitored on the "Project Status" link on the 
%Pt website.

| #14-10-2006, }%Pt and Tntnet at LinuxWorldExpo in Cologne}#\n
Platinum was presented together with Tntnet, the open-source web application server, at LinuxWorldExpo in Cologne in the open source pavillion.

| #22-09-2006, }%Pt on Sourceforge}#\n
The Platinum C++ framework is now hosted on sourceforge.net. The source code is available through an SVN repository and generated documentation can be viewed online now.

